WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.590
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:09.190
If you are new here, this is a series about
research with the name Two Minute Papers,

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.309
but let's be honest here.

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:13.099
It's never two minutes.

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:18.449
We are going to talk about two really cool
papers that help us create physically based

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:23.950
material models from photographs that we can
use in our light simulation programs.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:29.740
Just as a note, these authors, Miika and Jaakko
have been on a rampage for years now and have

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.890
popped so many fantastic papers each of which
I was blown away by.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:40.190
For instance, earlier, we talked about their
work on Gradient Domain Light Transport, brilliant

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:44.180
piece of work, I've put a link in the description
box, make sure to check it out!

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:49.269
So the problem we're trying to solve is very
simple to understand: the input is a photograph

00:00:49.269 --> 00:00:55.370
of a given material somewhere in our vicinity,
and the output is a bona fide physical material

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:59.329
model that we can use in our photorealistic
rendering program.

00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:03.910
We can import real world materials in our
virtual worlds, if you will.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:07.410
Before we proceed, let's define a few mandatory
terms:

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:13.830
A material is diffuse if incoming light from
one direction is reflected equally in all

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:14.830
directions.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:18.120
This means that they look the same from all
directions.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.040
White walls and matte surfaces are excellent
examples of that.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:27.970
A material we shall consider specular if incoming
light from one direction is reflected back

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:29.560
to one direction.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:33.890
This means that if we turn our head a bit,
we will see something different.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:39.390
For instance, the windshield of a car, water
and reflections in a mirror can be visualized

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.220
with a specular material model.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.810
Of course, materials can also be a combination
of both.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:51.320
For instance, car paint, our hair and skin
are all combinations of these material models.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.860
Glossy materials are midway between the two
where the incoming light from one direction

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:01.460
is reflected to not everywhere equally, and
not in one direction, but a small selected

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.110
set of directions.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.630
They change a bit when we move our head, but
not that much.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:12.519
In the Two-Shot Capture paper, a material
model is given by how much light is reflected

00:02:12.519 --> 00:02:18.239
and absorbed by the diffuse and specular components
of the material, and something that we call

00:02:18.239 --> 00:02:22.280
a normal map, which captures the bumpiness
of the material.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.510
Other factors like glossiness and anisotropy
are also recorded, but we shall focus on the

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.379
diffuse and the specular parts.

00:02:29.379 --> 00:02:35.930
The authors ask us to grab our phone for two
photographs of a material to ensure a high-quality

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:40.060
reconstruction procedure: one with flash,
and one without.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:44.000
And the question immediately arises: why two
images?

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.299
Well, the image without flash can capture
the component that looks the same from all

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:54.330
directions, this is the diffuse component,
and the photograph with flash can capture

00:02:54.330 --> 00:03:00.150
the specular component because we can see
how the material handles specular reflections.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:04.689
And it is needless to say, the presented results
are absolutely fantastic.

00:03:04.689 --> 00:03:09.540
So, first paper, two images, one material
model.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.120
And therein lies the problem, which they tried
to address in the second paper.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:20.760
If a computer looks at such an image, it doesn't
know which part of one photograph is the diffuse

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.739
and which is the specular reflection.

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:27.209
However, I remember sitting in the waiting
room of a hospital while reading the first

00:03:27.209 --> 00:03:33.510
paper, and this waiting room had a tiled glossy
wall, and I was thinking that one image should

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:39.040
be enough, because if I look at something,
I can easily discern what the diffuse colors

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.439
are, and which part is the specular reflection
of something else.

00:03:42.439 --> 00:03:45.180
I don't need multiple photographs for that.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.560
I can also immediately see how bumpy it is,
even from one photograph, I don't need to

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.319
turn my head around.

00:03:52.319 --> 00:03:58.519
This is because we, humans have not a mathematical,
but an intuitive understanding of the materials

00:03:58.519 --> 00:04:00.230
we see around us.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:05.999
So can we explain the same kind of understanding
of materials to a computer somehow?

00:04:05.999 --> 00:04:08.549
Can we do it with only one image?

00:04:08.549 --> 00:04:15.060
And the answer is, yes we can, and, hopefully,
we already feel the alluring call of neural

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.209
networks.

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:21.300
We can get a neural network that was trained
on a lot of different images to try to guess

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:24.690
what these material reflectance parameters
should look like.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:30.940
However, the output should not be one image,
but multiple images with the diffuse and specular

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:36.710
reflectance informations, and a normal map
to describe the bumpiness of this surface.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:41.520
Merely throwing a neural network at this problem,
is however, not sufficient.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.450
There needs to be some kind of conspiracy
between these images, because real materials

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:48.740
are not arbitrarily put together.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:54.130
If one of these images is smooth, or has interesting
features somewhere, the others have to follow

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:55.830
it in some way.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:05:01.040
This "some way" is mathematically quite challenging
to formulate, which is a really cool part

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.280
of the paper.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:07.970
This conspiracy part is a bit like if we had
4 criminals testifying at a trial, where they

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:13.180
try to sell their lies, and to maintain the
credibility of their made up story, they have

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:17.720
previously had to synchronize their lies so
they line up correctly.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:22.720
The paper contains neat tricks to control
the output of the neural network and create

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.980
these conspiracies across these multiple image
outputs that yield a valid and believable

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:29.460
material model.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.500
And the results, are again, just fantastic.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.840
Second paper, one image, one material model.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.760
It doesn't get any better than that.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:44.160
Spectacular, not specular...spectacular piece
of work.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:50.460
The first paper is great, but the second is
smoking hot, by all that is holy, I am getting

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:51.460
goosebumps.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:58.380
If you are interested in hearing a bit more
about light transport and are not afraid of

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:03.430
some mathematics, we recently recorded my
full course on this at the Technical University

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:08.050
of Vienna, the entirety of which is freely
available for everyone.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:12.550
There is a link for it in the video description
box, make sure to check it out!

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:33.610
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

